<body>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h1 style="font-size: 18pt; margin: 0;">Employee's Withholding Certificate</h1>
      <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p>OMB No. 15-045-0074</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
      <div style="width: 48%;">
        <div class="section-title">Step 1: Personal Information</div>
        <table style="margin-top: 10px;">
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 20%; vertical-align: top;">(a) First name and middle initial</td>
            <td style="width: 75%;">
              <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 20%; vertical-align: top;">Address</td>
            <td style="width: 75%;">
              <span data-field="address">{{ address }}</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 20%; vertical-align: top;">(b) Social security number</td>
            <td style="width: 75%;">
              <span data-field="ssn">{{ ssn }}</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div style="width: 48%; text-align: right;">
        <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
          <p><strong>20<span data-field="year">{{ year }}</span></strong></p>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
          <p>
            <input type="checkbox"> Does your name match the name on your social security card? If not, to ensure you get paid correctly, contact SSA at 800-772-1213 or go to www.ssa.gov.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <div class="section-title">Step 2: Multiple Jobs or Spouse Works</div>
      <p>Complete Steps 2-4 ONLY if you apply to these steps. Skip to Step 5. See page 2 for more information on each step, who can claim exemption from withholding, when to use the estimator at www.irs.gov/W4App, and privacy.</p>
      <p>
        <span data-field="step2_instruction">{{ step2_instruction }}</span>
      </p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p>Do only one of the following:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <span data-field="option1_text">{{ option1_text }}</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-field="option2_text">{{ option2_text }}</span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <div class="section-title">Step 3: Claim Dependents</div>
      <p>Complete Steps 3-4(b) on Form W-4 only if one of these jobs. Leave those steps blank for the other jobs. (Your withholding will be most accurate if you complete Steps 3-4(b) on the Form W-4 for the highest paying job.)</p>
      <p>
        <span data-field="step3_instruction">{{ step3_instruction }}</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <div class="section-title">Step 4: Other Adjustments</div>
      <p>
        <span data-field="step4_instruction">{{ step4_instruction }}</span>
      </p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p>(a) Other income (not from jobs). If you want tax withheld for other income you expect this year that won't have withholding, enter the amount of other income here. This may include interest, dividends, and retirement income.</p>
        <table style="margin-top: 10px;">
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 50%;"></td>
            <td style="width: 20%; text-align: right;">4(a) $</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <p>(b) Deductions. If you expect to claim deductions other than the standard deduction and want to reduce your withholding, use the Deductions Worksheet on page 3 and enter the result here.</p>
        <table style="margin-top: 10px;">
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 50%;"></td>
            <td style="width: 20%; text-align: right;">4(b) $</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <p>(c) Extra withholding. Enter any additional tax you want withheld each pay period.</p>
        <table style="margin-top: 10px;">
          <tr>
            <td style="width: 50%;"></td>
            <td style="width: 20%; text-align: right;">4(c) $</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <div class="section-title">Step 5: Sign Here</div>
      <p>Under penalties of perjury, I declare that this certificate, to the best of my knowledge and belief, is true, correct, and complete.</p>
      <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 50px;">
        <div style="width: 45%;">
          <p>Employee's signature (This form is not valid unless you sign it.)</p>
          <div style="height: 30px; border-bottom: 1px solid black;" data-field="step_5_sign_here">{{ step_5_sign_here }}</div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 45%; text-align: right;">
          <p>Date</p>
          <div style="height: 30px; border-bottom: 1px solid black;" data-field="date">{{ date }}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
      <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 45%;">
          <p>Employers Only</p>
          <p>Employer's name and address</p>
          <div style="height: 30px; border-bottom: 1px solid black;" data-field="employer_s_name_and_address">{{ employer_s_name_and_address }}</div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 45%; text-align: right;">
          <p>First date of employment</p>
          <div style="height: 30px; border-bottom: 1px solid black;" data-field="first_date_of_employment">{{ first_date_of_employment }}</div>
          <p>Employer identification number (EIN)</p>
          <div style="height: 30px; border-bottom: 1px solid black;" data-field="employer_identification_number">{{ employer_identification_number }}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt;">General Instructions</h2>
      <p>Section references are to the Internal Revenue Code.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt;">Specific Instructions</h2>
      <p>Step 1(c). Check your anticipated filing status. This will determine the standard deduction and tax rates used to compute your withholding.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">Future Developments</div>
    <p>For the latest information about developments related to Form W-4, such as legislation enacted after it was published, go to www.irs.gov/FormW4.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">Purpose of Form</div>
    <p>Complete Form W-4 so that your employer can withhold the correct federal income tax from your pay. If too little is withheld, you will generally owe tax when you file your tax return and may owe a penalty. If too much is withheld, you will generally receive a refund. This form is used to tell your employer how much tax to withhold from your paycheck. For more information on withholding and when you must furnish a new Form W-4, see www.irs.gov/W4App.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">Exemption from Withholding</div>
    <p>You may claim exemption from withholding for 2022 if you meet both of the following conditions: you had no federal income tax liability in 2022 and you expect to have no federal income tax liability in 2021.</p>
    <p>If you meet both conditions, you can check the box in Step 1(c) on Form W-4. If you claim exemption, you will have no income tax withheld from your paycheck and may owe taxes and penalties when you file your 2022 tax return. If you do not meet both conditions, you must complete all of Form W-4.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">Your Privacy</div>
    <p>If you prefer to limit information provided in Step 2, use the online estimator, which will also increase accuracy.</p>
    <p>As an alternative to the estimator, if you have concerns about your privacy, you may check the box in Step 2(b) if you are not claiming dependents.</p>
    <p>If you check the box in Step 2(b), you may instead check the box in Step 1(c), which will increase your paycheck often by thousands of dollars over the year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">When to use the estimator</div>
    <p>Consider using the estimator at www.irs.gov/W4App if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>You work only to the part year.</li>
      <li>You have dividend or capital gain income, or are subject to additional taxes, such as Additional Medicare Tax.</li>
      <li>You have self-employment income (see below); or</li>
      <li>You want the most accurate withholding for multiple jobs.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div class="section-title">Self-employment. Generally, you will owe both income and self-employment taxes on any self-employment income you receive separate from the wages you receive as an employee. If you want to pay these taxes, you must have tax withheld from your wages, use the estimator at www.irs.gov/W4App to figure the amount to have withheld.</div>
  </div>
</div>

